i have the pleasure of joining me right  now to my nhl player inclusion committee  teammates  that stanley cup champion kevin  shattenkirk  current member of the anaheim ducks  and also olympic champion  megan duggan  currently the manager of player  development with new jersey devils  welcome to you both good to see you  thanks for having us hanson good to see  you as well  i thought it was important to have both  you guys on and  i think both you guys thought it was  even more important than i did to have  this conversation because  we're all frustrated with our pic  over the events the last week first we  had martin luther king day junior which  we celebrated and we had the raising of  willie louise number 22 to the raptors  in boston but right after that we had  two racial incidents one  in the ahl the other one the echl that  really felt like it set our game back  why did both of you feel that was  important to have this conversation make  sure that your voices were heard  megan we'll start with you  yeah sure and and anson thanks for um  thanks for having the discussion with us  um today i think  for me i mean it's passion like i'm i  have a passion for amplifying this  message and supporting uh  you know my fellow players of color in  our sport it's it's on all of us um you  know inclusion acceptance um and it's on  us and  it's it's our game to grow and really  protect and honor  um and and i personally certainly i take  a lot of pride in in being a member of  our player inclusion committee i've  learned a lot um i've grown a lot in  being in the group  but i know myself kevin the members of  the other members of the committee  we take a lot of pride moving to action  um engaging in incidents like this here  here having our voices heard um  incidents continue like this to sadly  present themselves and  these are things that as a hockey  community we have to educate ourselves  around and we have to work to educate  others on  and when it comes to something like this  i i don't think words are enough i don't  think  posting on instagram is enough  and and personally i want to act i want  to um engage in an allyship or ants and  as we've talked about co  co-conspiracy um in order to to help act  and and move our sport away from um  incidents like this  i think  megan hit it on the head there the the  passion for the game i think is  something that we all share uh it's it's  nothing that  uh changes from  person to person color to color um male  female we all love this game for the  same reason um  but unfortunately there are certain  players who have to deal with  different obstacles uh in order to to  play the game that they love and i think  that's what we're all trying to do here  is make this  a welcoming place for anyone and i think  you know our landing our voices  hopefully shows that there are some  allies here for  uh for the black players who who  obviously had to go through that post  incidents this weekend um for females  who  are trying to break into the game and  and have to go into a locker room with  uh with a couple of young boy players in  order to just get out on the ice i think  it's it's important that they know that  we are standing by them uh that they do  have people in the sport who  support them and who are trying to  change and change our game for the  better in order for  these incidents to hopefully disappear  and never come around again  shady you're a parent and also you're  very instrumental with the players  sitting out processing a couple years  ago with fellow pic team ryan reeves and  you guys are both in the bubble  what conversations as your children  start to get older would you have with  them uh if and you know they decide to  play the game of hockey  well i i think  you know the  the way that i was raised  you know by my parents was  um  you know that we  we need to treat everyone with respect  uh you know that that's top to bottom it  doesn't matter um  what your sexual orientation is what  color you are it really uh that has no  no place in our home um and i would just  do that do just that i mean hopefully  leading by example and showing my kids  how i treat others uh is is the first  way that they see that but  um you know coming into the sport of  hockey i think hopefully they can see  uh if they do that that  um  you know i was passionate about this and  and i i always will be and it's  something that uh you know i expect of  them as well as to be  great people and i think that's that's  what this all comes down to is how do we  become great people and and better for  our community  you guys have both worn the red white  and blue the olympics are right around  the corner and mags you sit on the board  of usa hockey  how do we start to really accelerate  that growth within our grassroots of  hockey here in the united states because  we feel like it's systematic right you  see these one-offs happening all the  time but it continues to happen and it's  not going to change overnight  what do you think we should do to try to  accelerate that growth within minor  hockey  yeah um you know anson sadly we're still  seeing incidents um such as these that  we're discussing today and in the  grassroots of hockey and um you know i  think in one way um  how how we as a sport handle incidents  such as as this  how we provide  learning experiences and education um  you know to the hockey community after  incidents like this at the top  um you know we hope that that that that  shows um you know to young players  you know to the hockey ecosystem  that these types of things you know they  violate the sanctity of our game um and  and they're not they're not welcome um  and you hope that those types of things  send a message  um but in addition to that i mean i  think um  again getting into action like our group  our player inclusion committee  continuing to  get out into different spaces  in the hockey ecosystem when we can you  know within usa hockey within  all different community programs and  having these discussions you know um  bringing this type of um of of learning  experience education the training um  into locker rooms into  um boards into teams um so that  people slowly start to  understand uh how how we need to behave  as a sport um and what's tolerated and  what's not um and then you know continue  to to police these kind of things um  educate parents around it and when they  see these things in ranks uh they need  to have discussions with kids um and at  a usa hockey level you know i know that  in the last couple years  there's been things that have been done  at that level where they're trying to  increase their education and their  training around um tolerance and  inclusion and acceptance  but still have a long way to go and and  i'm certainly um you know i'm excited to  be a proud to be a part of that group in  order to keep pushing the needle and  hammering home some of this messaging  and now that you're in the hockey upside  there with the new jersey devils they're  meg's what conversations are you guys  having on the team side when it comes to  these incidents popping up all the time  because it affected  pk suban indirectly because one of his  siblings jordan was one of the people  that you know suffered from this  incident in the echo  yeah i mean similar kind of thing again  just the importance of of having  discussions bringing these things to  light talking about them uh making sure  that everyone's educated i'm proud to be  a part of the devils because i feel it's  a um you know it's an inclusive  welcoming environment um you know for me  and on my side my family i'm a gay woman  that's been invited into um you know to  hockey operations and and that's rare in  itself and so um again i think just  continuing to not shy away from having  these conversations  um and to challenging each other on the  way that we speak to each other how  um you know certain gestures are are  perceived and what they mean um you know  and i think  all of us have um you know we have a  responsibility to educate ourselves and  to educate those that we work with  and to continue to  move our sport um ahead and away from  being perceived uh as a place that's  that's not welcoming to a certain amount  of communities  and shady are you guys having these  similar conversations in the room you  guys are current players right now i  know it's easy for former players like  myself to have these conversations we  have a lot of time on our hands but as  current players you guys are currently  in the fight right now uh you guys have  these conversations paying attention  what's happening at the east coast level  in the ahl level  absolutely i think uh  this is our sport right so it's it's  something that affects all of us and  it  saddens us you know that  uh players have to go through  situations like that um we have guys on  our team who  compete and and and played against boko  hama um you know last year i think in in  uh in the ahl and and i remember talking  to uh to one of them buddy robinson and  and  you know he he just talked about how  really just terrible he felt for boco  this is the second time that he's had to  go through this  and talk about jordan and a lot of guys  obviously you know pk and  um  you know i played against pk growing up  in  minor hockey  i never would expect to do something  like that to to anyone um but if that's  something that unfortunately  i'm sure he had to deal with and his  brothers had to deal with and you had to  deal with anton growing up as a young  player and and i think that's what  really hits home for us as uh in our  locker room is that  um  our children  are gonna have to deal with this uh and  and we don't want young kids to have to  go through these situations how can we  be better and how can we help change  this so that um the next generation of  hockey players can  [Music]  hopefully bring this sport to a place  that uh is completely free and and  you know there's no judgment being  assessed by anyone  i can't thank you both enough you guys  have been unbelievable teammates  tremendous allies i appreciate you both  for sharing your voice because i think  hockey fans and parents and other  players need to see people like  yourselves or white allies that are  behind this that are disgusted and want  to see this end i think people  will see me enough times and players of  color will speak out about these things  but it's about time that we have allies  like yourselves that you know are  confident enough uh to be able to lend  your voice for support because that's  going to be so critical for us to  continue to change our game and make our  game a better place so thank you both  appreciate it

